{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Observation\n\nA `make verify` on arm64 macOS (2026-10-27) failed 22 tests plus 1 error, all in 4 files that drive `infra/scripts/observability.sh` (`test_observability_mcp_health_9ea3.py`, `_offsets_6a65.py`, `_marker_anchor_8c2f.py`, `_mcp_markers.py`). Each failing case raised `subprocess.TimeoutExpired` after the tests' 60 s bound; stderr also named `/var/lib/rebar/probe-region` and `/var/lib/rebar/reviewbot-webhook-auth-offset` as missing. Reproduction evidence is in the comments.\n\n## Root cause\n\n1. **The tests reach the host's real Docker daemon with no bound.** The script calls `docker system df`, `docker ps` and `docker stats --no-stream` through `clamped`/`bounded`. On a host without coreutils `timeout` (stock macOS), `bounded` deliberately runs the command unbounded. 12 of the 19 test files that run the whole script do not stub `docker`, so each run waits on the host daemon: measured 6 s (`system df`) + 3 s (`stats`) per run on colima, 16–17 s per script run versus 5 s with `docker` stubbed. Under load this exceeds 60 s; a `docker` stub that stalls reproduces the exact `TimeoutExpired` signature.\n2. **Timeouts orphan the script.** `subprocess.run(..., timeout=...)` kills only the direct `bash`; the script's subshells and their children survive at PPID 1 (16 orphaned pairs measured after the 2026-10-27 run; reproduced here).\n3. **No test redirects every host state path.** The script defaults 16 state paths to `/var/lib/rebar/...` (`NAME=\"${NAME:-/var/lib/rebar/...}\"`). Every one of the 19 files leaves between 4 and 16 of them unset, so runs try to read and write the real host directory. On this host the writes fail silently (`2>/dev/null || true`), so this is not what times out — but a host where `/var/lib/rebar` exists (the production layout) would have test runs read and overwrite real offset state, and nothing stops a new path being added unredirected.\n\nThe missing-path stderr lines are a symptom, not the cause of the timeout: the script continues and exits 0.\n\n## Approach (test-side; production defaults unchanged)\n\nScope: all 19 files under `tests/scripts/test_observability_*.py` that run the whole script. The other 2 (`_cap_deps`, `_pct_of_cap_b380`) only read the script text or run one extracted function, and are left as they are.\n\n1. Add one shared helper module `tests/scripts/_observability_harness.py`:\n   - `state_path_defaults()` parses the script for every `NAME=\"${NAME:-/var/lib/rebar/...}\"` default, so a path added to the script is redirected without editing any test.\n   - `observability_env(tmp_path)` returns the subprocess environment with every such variable pointing into `tmp_path`, and a fallback `docker` stub (`exit 1`, i.e. unavailable) placed ahead of the host `PATH`. A test's own `bin/` stubs are prepended after it, so tests that stub `docker` keep their stub. Test-specific overrides still win because tests update the env afterwards.\n   - `run_observability(argv, env=..., timeout=...)` starts the script in its own session and, on timeout, kills the whole process group and reaps it before re-raising `TimeoutExpired`. Before starting, it refuses (AssertionError naming the variable and its default) any env in which a state variable still resolves under `/var/lib/rebar`, so an unredirected path fails fast in under a second instead of timing out.\n2. Convert the 19 files to build their env with `observability_env` and run the script with `run_observability`. No assertion, stub or scenario changes; existing per-file timeouts (60/120/180/300 s) are kept.\n3. Add the guard test `tests/scripts/test_observability_harness_isolation.py`:\n   - every non-comment `/var/lib/rebar` literal in the script sits in a parsed `${NAME:-...}` default (a hard-coded path fails and names its line);\n   - the parse finds all 16 current defaults;\n   - no test file in `tests/scripts/` runs `observability.sh` (or a copy) through `subprocess` directly — every whole-script run goes through `run_observability` (AST scan; names file and line);\n   - `run_observability` rejects an env missing one state variable, naming it;\n   - an induced timeout (stub that sleeps) leaves no surviving `observability.sh` process.\n4. Do not change `infra/scripts/observability.sh`, raise the 60 s bound, skip, or mark any test platform-specific.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Every `/var/lib/rebar` default in `infra/scripts/observability.sh` is redirected into `tmp_path` for every whole-script run in `tests/scripts/`, enforced by `run_observability` refusing an unredirected env and by the guard test naming any unparsed literal.\n- [ ] `docker` is stubbed for every whole-script run (fallback stub, or the test's own).\n- [ ] On timeout no `observability.sh` process survives; the guard test proves it with an induced timeout.\n- [ ] `pytest tests/scripts/test_observability_*.py tests/scripts/test_observability_harness_isolation.py` passes on arm64 macOS with no manual host setup, and `/var/lib/rebar` does not exist on the host afterwards.\n- [ ] `pytest tests/scripts/` passes, and `make lint` and `make typecheck` exit 0.\n- [ ] `infra/scripts/observability.sh` is unchanged.\n- [ ] No test is skipped or marked platform-specific.\n- [ ] Per-run time with real docker versus the stub is recorded on this ticket, before and after.\n\nNot in scope: bug `degraded-chilly-galago` (pandoc on arm64), which is the other blocker for a green `make verify` on arm64 macOS."}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1793488685594694001,"uuid":"dd6e0a29-14d3-4e73-94b7-2a7cda572637"}